Declarations are private to their module by default. In `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth`, place `$export` on its own line before a declaration to include it in that facade's public API.

### Exporting declarations

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- @mod.moth
    private_helper || -> String:
        return "private"
    ;

    $export
    public_name #= "Moth"

    $export
    render || -> String:
        return private_helper()
    ;
]

`public_name` and `render` are visible through the module's normal facade. `private_helper` remains private. An unmarked facade exports nothing.

`$export` applies to the next eligible top-level item only. It must occupy its own line, with no operands. Comment-only lines may intervene, but a blank line breaks attachment. It is valid only in the two facade files, including when both exist in one directory. It does not apply in `@entry.moth`, ordinary helper files or `config.moth`.

The directive can mark a function, struct, choice, transparent type alias, trait or compile-time `#` constant. The `#` marker controls when a constant is evaluated, not whether it is public.

Runtime bindings, mutable bindings, executable statements, templates, page fragments, trait conformances and incompatibilities cannot be exported. Receiver methods are exposed through their public receiver type, not exported independently.

### Re-exporting dependencies

A facade may publish selected declarations defined in another file without moving them into the facade:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export
    @components/card CardData as Card, render_card

    $export
    @core/math PI, sin
]

One `$export` publishes every selected name in its dependency clause. The alias `Card` is the public name. `CardData` is not also published unless selected separately. Bare namespaces and namespace aliases cannot be re-exported.

An ordinary dependency clause without `$export` binds names for the facade's own use but does not publish them. See @../packages/#public-reexports (Public re-exports) for selection and alias rules.

### Visibility boundaries

Files in one module can bind shared helper declarations through ordinary dependencies. Declarations authored directly in any root file remain local to that root. Other roots and helper files cannot read them, even if the roots share a directory.

Consumers in another module can bind only the applicable facade's explicitly exported names. A normal module publishes through `@mod.moth`. A scoped support or external project package publishes through `@package.moth`. Resolution never falls back between facades or bypasses one with a filesystem path.

See @#module-roots (Module roots) for file roles and @../packages/#module-visibility (Module visibility) for the complete access rules.

### Public API contracts

An exported signature must not expose private types, inaccessible trait requirements or project-private context. Exported functions and public receiver methods also need closed error contracts: implicit built-in failures must be handled or delivered through an explicit built-in `Error!` result. A custom error result needs local recovery or explicit mapping. The same rules apply to re-exports.

See @../functions/#returns-and-multiple-values (Returns and multiple values) and @../errors/#propagation (Error propagation) for error handling.

